# zstack-verify

## Pick checks from the diff

Always run the adjacent suites for the files you changed, then the static
gates: `pnpm typecheck`, `pnpm lint`, `pnpm check:ui`,
`pnpm test:git` (filtered to the touched area), `pnpm check:secrets`.

| Touched | Also run |
|---|---|
| Renderer UI, styling or tokens | `pnpm check:ui`; after a token or contrast change, `pnpm design:docs` |
| Frames in `Zeros - Design/` | `design validate` and `design capture` from the frame context, then inspect the PNG. Add `--theme light` to capture Light; the default is the Dark theme. |
| Composer, overlay, focus or popover | `pnpm test:ui-smoke`. Label the PR `ci:ui-smoke`. |
| Performance-sensitive UI | exact-key and race tests, `pnpm build:ui` |
| Electron IPC or preload | `pnpm check:preload` and the relevant Electron tests |
| `apps/control-plane/` | `pnpm test:control-plane`, `pnpm --dir apps/control-plane typecheck` |
| `.github/workflows/` | `pnpm check:actions` (owner merge required) |
| Migrations, packaging, protocol or deploy paths | the matching `check:*` scripts in `package.json` |
| Runtime dependencies or packaged assets | `pnpm check:licenses` |
| Engine lifecycle | `pnpm smoke:engine`, on macOS only |
| Control-plane concurrency, locks or transactions | Label the PR `ci:control-plane-db`. PR CI is path-selected, and without the label the full CP database lane runs only after merge, where a timing race turns `main` red. |

CI's full root and control-plane suites are the authority. Locally, run the
adjacent suites plus the gates above; don't wait on a full local run before
handing off. Preview the CI lanes with `pnpm ci:plan`.

The selector can do this bookkeeping:

```sh
node scripts/zstack/verify.mjs plan --test path/to/adjacent.test.ts
node scripts/zstack/verify.mjs run --test path/to/adjacent.test.ts --execute --receipt /private/scratch/verify.json
```

Record `--mode single-session --agent <model>`, or `--mode master-subagents
--agent <model> --master <model>`, on the command. The selector uses the shared
CI registry; unknown CI command text is deferred, never executed as shell.
An incomplete diff or missing adjacent suite blocks execution. A source change
during verification invalidates the receipt. The local receipt is unsigned
engineering evidence; CI is still the merge authority.

`node scripts/zstack/verify.mjs local-ui` plans the existing Linux app driver.
Add `--execute --receipt <file>` to run it, optionally with `--shard 1/3`.
Remaining shards and macOS proof remain unverified. See `agents/zstack/tools.md`
for limits and the closed error codes.

## Live cloud checks on Alpha

Cloud workspaces run one Boat VM each. The control plane connects in to the
worker through a protected Boat port route; the worker keeps outbound HTTPS for
history, activity and credentials. Before a live run, run the offline suites:

- `apps/cloud-worker` (`pnpm --dir apps/cloud-worker test`);
- `pnpm test:control-plane`, with real PostgreSQL for store tests;
- the shared renderer suites under `platform/bridge`.

The branch's live proof uses the isolated local stack and gate0 probe:

```sh
pnpm electron:cloud --headless --runtime <runtime-sha256>
pnpm --dir apps/control-plane cloud-v2:gate0 --local --runtime <runtime-sha256>
```

- Run it only with the task's cloud-provider authorization, against this
  checkout's backend whose `CLOUD_V2_RUNTIME_SHA256` matches `--runtime`.
  `electron:cloud` accepts the pin through `--runtime` or
  `ZEROS_CLOUD_V2_RUNTIME_SHA256`; no Railway service or PlanetScale branch is
  created. See `docs/electron-cloud.md`.
- It creates one disposable workspace from the existing Cloud Computer. It
  runs native Claude, Codex and Cursor turns, plus terminal, file and Git
  round trips. It reads the mirrored history while the VM is stopped, wakes
  the VM once, then deletes the workspace. Pass `--keep` only when you will
  delete the workspace yourself.
- It starts a real VM. Run it once per tested change; never loop it.
- It reports the failing stage only. Before any cleanup, capture the
  server-side state: control-plane logs and the workspace row through the API.
- Provider keys stay in `.env.agent` (Alpha and test only) or Actions; never
  copy them into the checkout or into output.

## Evidence rules

- Report counts, for example "adjacent 130/130 across 11 suites". Name what you
  did not run and why.
- A retry never relabels the original failure. Record both results.
- Every time in a record, plan or report comes from a clock read, never an
  estimate: write it with `$(date -u +%H:%M)` inside the command that appends it.
- Don't claim macOS-only checks from Linux.

## Gotchas already paid for

- A pipe through `sed`/`grep` buffers output. Use `sed -u` or
  `--line-buffered` when a later step needs a line immediately, such as a URL
  the owner must open.
- Time windows: tolerate clock skew between this host and Alpha (about 0.5 s
  observed). Compare against the documented bound, never an exact value.
- Harness input files must be regular files you own, mode 0600 and small.
- When a check fails, first rule out whether the environment changed under you
  (PATH tools, database encoding, a disk precondition) before blaming the code.
- A workspace whose setup ran only `pnpm install` fails `pnpm typecheck` in
  `apps/control-plane` with missing modules. `scripts/dev-environment/hook.sh setup`
  installs all three dependency graphs, as `.conductor/settings.toml` does.
  Playwright-backed Design suites also need `npx playwright install chromium`.
  Before blaming your diff for a red local run, rerun the failing files in a
  clean checkout of your base commit. A base worktree at another path changes
  path- and Git-dependent suites; for an exact comparison, swap only your
  changed source files back to the base revision in the same checkout, compare
  failing test names, then rerun every differing test in isolation.
- In a shared working tree, qualify a frozen patch on its stated base in a
  separate worktree or disposable projection, with workspace dependency links
  bound to that tree. Record its SHA-256 and test evidence; never replace
  concurrent source edits in place to reconstruct the patch.
- An install with `--ignore-scripts` leaves `node-pty` without its native
  binding. Run `pnpm --dir apps/cloud-worker rebuild node-pty` before rerunning
  worker PTY tests; keep the prerequisite failure in the evidence.
- Run protocol suites from the repository root, for example
  `timeout 900 pnpm exec vitest run packages/protocol/src/__tests__/cloud-v2-relay.test.ts`.
  Package-local Vitest inherits root-relative includes and may find no tests;
  that result is unverified.
- `pnpm typecheck` keeps incremental state in `node_modules/.cache/zeros-typecheck`.
  An error in a file you did not touch may be stale state: confirm with
  `pnpm exec tsgo --noEmit -p tsconfig.typecheck.json --incremental false` before chasing it.
  `typecheck:app` and `typecheck:electron` run the native TypeScript 7 compiler
  (`tsgo`); editors, ESLint and compiler-API scripts stay on `typescript` 5.x,
  so keep code valid under both. Its DOM types are narrower (for example
  `WebSocket.readyState` is `0 | 1 | 2 | 3`): re-read state after an `await`
  through a method instead of a narrowed property.
- On macOS the default `TMPDIR` (`/var/folders/…`) is reached through the
  `/var` symlink, so path-safety suites fail with `zstack_source_unsafe`,
  `zstack_receipt_unsafe` or "not canonical". Rerun them with `TMPDIR` set to a
  directory under `/private/tmp`.
- Outside CI, Vitest reuses transformed modules from
  `node_modules/.experimental-vitest-cache` between runs. If a rerun behaves
  differently from a fresh checkout, clear it with `pnpm exec vitest --clearCache`
  before chasing the difference.
- `pnpm test:ui-smoke` stops at the first scenario that throws, so one base
  failure hides every later scenario. Confirm the failure on the base commit,
  then run the rest from an untracked copy of `scripts/` without that scenario
  (registry and partition), with the affected scenarios in one shard; delete
  the copy afterwards.
- `pnpm check:runtime-skew` reads its pinned commits from local Git objects
  with `git --no-lazy-fetch`, which needs Git 2.44 or later. A shallow clone,
  or Git 2.43 (Ubuntu 24.04), reports `runtime_skew_source_unavailable`. Fetch
  full history and check `git --version` before suspecting the pins.
- Work that holds a pooled connection across provider I/O (Cloud Computer
  builds hold an advisory-lock connection per build) must leave one
  connection free in its role's pool; the worker pool has two. Test concurrent
  jobs at that size.
- A new advisory lock needs one `/* lock_purpose='…' */` tag registered in
  `ADVISORY_LOCK_PURPOSES`; only `src/database-lock-tags.test.ts` checks it.
- Never point an ad-hoc run at a test database a running suite uses: global
  setup resets the schema. Rerun any overlapping failure alone first.
- Database-backed control-plane suites skip without `TEST_DATABASE_URL`. Give
  each concurrent agent its own PostgreSQL 18 server, not only its own
  database: migration 0004 creates the cluster-wide `zeros_app` role with a
  check-then-create race. Without Docker or sudo, signature-verified PGDG
  packages unpacked with `dpkg -x` run as an unprivileged user. On Amazon
  Linux, install `postgresql18-server` and `postgresql18-contrib`: without
  contrib, migration 0001 fails creating `citext` (SQLSTATE 0A000).
- Parallel schema resets in separate databases on one retained test server
  still share PostgreSQL's lock table. Size `max_locks_per_transaction` for
  that concurrency before sharding; `out of shared memory` at reset is a
  fixture failure, not a product regression.
- Materialize a limited `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` claim set before updating
  leases. Test the actual returned bound with zero-duration leases and apply
  in-flight exclusions before the limit; a re-evaluated subquery can overclaim.
- PostgreSQL timestamps retain microseconds that JavaScript `Date` drops.
  Round-tripped timestamp compare-and-set predicates must match the token's
  precision, or use an exact database revision; test a database-origin value.
- Provider intent must survive Stop, deletion and a lost reply. Persist its
  identity and deadline before I/O, then test reconciliation with the same key
  before cleanup can declare the resource gone.
- Adding columns to an existing table also requires the control plane's
  `src/migrations.test.ts`. Whole-row ladder comparisons must project the
  original columns to check preservation and verify new defaults separately.
- A recovery change to a shared provider method must be opt-in for callers
  with durable retry identity. Test other callers too: retention can resume a
  named snapshot, while a new Computer build must not reuse an old `-v1` name
  after a development database reset.
- CI's PostgreSQL runs in a service container in another PID namespace, so a
  test cannot signal or inspect the server's processes. Reproduce connection
  races at the protocol layer, for example an in-test TCP relay that holds
  client bytes, and assert that the race window was actually open.
- In a sparse checkout (Working folders), `git checkout -b` and `checkout -m`
  overwrite an untracked file at a path the target adds and only warn, and
  `git read-tree <tree>` clears skip-worktree bits so excluded folders look
  deleted. Test branch switches in a sparse checkout too, and restore an index
  with `git reset -q <tree> -- .`.
- Adding or renaming a file under an audited directory such as `docs/` also
  needs the expected list in `scripts/__tests__/repository-layout.test.ts`.
  `pnpm ci:plan` only selects the lane, so run that test before pushing. When
  two open PRs each add a document, the second to merge adds both.
